commit 865963e3880236d64c426037816b218cb6c159a8
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 21 15:18:00 2026 -0700

    Build against mlx-swift main (#469): Parakeet and Qwen3-TTS compile calls (#292)
    
    * Parakeet: store compiled closures without @Sendable
    
    mlx-swift#469 changed compile(inputs:) to return a non-Sendable closure,
    because the closure captures the modules passed as inputs. ParakeetModel
    stored both compiled closures as @Sendable, so main stops compiling at
    ParakeetModel.swift:326 and :909 against mlx-swift main. Package.swift
    accepts any 0.x from 0.32.3, so the next mlx-swift release would break
    every fresh resolve.
    
    Neither closure leaves the model, so the annotation bought nothing.
    Dropping it builds against both 0.32.3 and mlx-swift main.
    
    * Qwen3-TTS: call MLX.compile for the SwiGLU kernels
    
    mlx-swift#469 added compile(shapeless:_:) for two-argument @Sendable
    closures to MLX, with the same signature as MLXAudioCore's compile shim.
    Qwen3TTSCodePredictor.swift:9 and Qwen3TTSTalker.swift:34 import both
    modules, so against mlx-swift main the bare call is ambiguous and
    MLXAudioTTS stops compiling. Naming MLX picks the same function the shim
    forwards to, on 0.32.3 and on main.

commit b7dd2e0c93065d15c860864ba3cbccab565f4d07
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 21 15:17:46 2026 -0700

    Nemotron streaming: reuse decoder and encoder work that has not changed (#275)
    
    * Nemotron RNN-T decode: reuse the prediction network output on blank frames
    
    The prediction network output, its proposed LSTM state and the joint's pred
    projection depend only on (lastToken, decoderState), which change only on
    emission. Cache them and clear the cache on every emission, so a blank frame
    costs one enc projection and the joint output layer instead of an LSTM step too.
    Applies to the stream decode and the offline decode; the offline loop also
    drops the eval before .item(), which already evaluates the joint output.
    
    Idea from achembarpu (be2beeb, a60b91a). His cache was keyed on lastToken,
    which reuses a stale output when the same token is emitted twice in a row;
    clearing on emission avoids that.
    
    * Nemotron stream encoder: build per-chunk invariants once
    
    posEnc depends only on chunk length, cache length and dtype, which are the
    same for every layer within a chunk, so layers share one embedding. The
    prompt one-hot depends only on (batch, chunk length, dtype, prompt index), so
    the stream state reuses it for every whole chunk. The 0.5 residual scale is
    cast once per chunk instead of twice per layer, recast if a layer's dtype
    differs. Same values, same ops downstream.
    
    * Nemotron streaming: skip the text rebuild when a step emits no token
    
    The full text is rebuilt from every token so far after each step and chunk,
    so a long session costs O(tokens) host work per step. With no new token the
    rebuilt text equals the previous one and the delta is empty, so skip it.
    
    * Nemotron RNN-T stream decode: read tokens for 4 frames per host sync
    
    With the prediction cached, the tokens of the next frames only depend on
    their encoder frame until an emission. Build each frame's joint and argmax
    as before (separate M=1 ops, same inputs), read up to 4 of them back with one
    asArray instead of one .item() per frame, and drop the rest on emission.
    Blank runs need a quarter of the syncs; an emission wastes at most 3 joint
    evaluations.
    
    * Nemotron streaming: cache segments with the text; one optional for the prediction cache
    
    The segments getter rebuilt every sentence on each access. The alignment is
    now rebuilt only when a step adds a token, and segments are built once from
    it. The stream prediction cache is one optional tuple, like the offline one,
    and the offline loop drops its force unwraps.
    
    * Test: Nemotron stream RNN-T decode matches per-frame recompute
    
    The per-frame reference recomputes the prediction network on every frame.
    The random tiny model can predict blank everywhere or nowhere, so a seeded
    calibration shifts the joint's blank bias and pushes special tokens down
    until the reference has blank frames, emitted tokens and a frame with 2+
    symbols. Covers emissions inside a 4-frame sync batch and a short last batch.
    The offline decode must match the cache-aware stream; that test only needs
    blank frames and emitted tokens, since no bias shift gave a 2+ symbol frame
    for the bf16 offline input on Metal CI.
    
    * Nemotron streaming: evaluate the cached prompt one-hot; say why the cached prediction isn't

