commit ba6e46c10b8b1a50cb0a4f81bcd4f20be6dc316d
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 21 14:44:15 2026 -0700

    VoxtralRealtime generateStream: build deltas from token bytes (#273)
    
    * VoxtralRealtime generateStream: build deltas from token bytes
    
    generateStream re-decoded the whole transcript on every token to find the
    new text, so a stream of n tokens cost O(n^2) decoding. It now appends each
    token's bytes to VoxtralRealtimeTranscriptText, as the stream session does,
    and yields the same deltas, including for tokens that split a multi-byte
    character.
    
    The decode loops also take the argmax together with the decoder step, one
    GPU sync per token instead of two; build the RoPE inverse frequencies once
    at init instead of on every forward pass; and skip the decoder pass after
    the last position, whose prediction nothing reads. Token ids are unchanged.
    
    * VoxtralRealtime: address review on #273
    
    evalPrediction's callers all pass one row from decoder.logits, and
    argMax(axis: -1) gives a single element for [vocab] and [1, vocab]
    alike, so the rank check goes. ropeInvFreq now evaluates the array it
    returns, since the decoder caches it.

commit d36caf92e125db11308b81ffd983b4504e24551c
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 21 14:37:43 2026 -0700

    VoxtralRealtime streaming: reuse the mel Hann window instead of rebuilding it per append (#271)
    
    * perf: reuse the Voxtral streaming Hann window
    
    melColumns rebuilt the 400-point window on every append: an arange, a cast,
    a cosine and two arithmetic ops per 100 ms of audio. Hoist it into
    periodicHannWindow(size:) and cache one on VoxtralRealtimeMelStream.
    
    [tom: made the window parameter required rather than optional, which leaves
    melColumns with one code path and makes its windowSize parameter dead]
    
    * test(voxtral): cover the periodic Hann window definition
    
    Checks periodicHannWindow against the formula and asserts the last sample is
    non-zero, which a symmetric N-1 window would not satisfy.
    
    * test(voxtral): add an opt-in benchmark for the window rebuild
    
    Times melColumns with a rebuilt window against a reused one in one process,
    plus a full melStream.append for the denominator. Off unless
    VOXTRAL_MEL_BENCH=1, and it needs xcodebuild: swift test has no metallib.
    
    * VoxtralRealtime: evaluate the Hann window when it is built
    
    The mel stream keeps the window for the whole session; evaluating it in
    periodicHannWindow means the first append doesn't pay for building it.
    
    ---------
    
    Co-authored-by: Arvind Chembarpu <achembarpu@gmail.com>

commit 624fd5410b2fc8ea06f955500c9ac078e405261f
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 21 08:18:36 2026 -0700

    Nemotron streaming: compute only new mel frames; keep MLX's buffer cache between steps (#274)
    
    * Nemotron stream: keep MLX's buffer cache between steps
    
    advance() called Memory.clearCache() after every step, so each step handed
    its buffers back to Metal and allocated them again. Steps now reuse the
    cached buffers; the cache is cleared once when the stream finishes.
    
    * Nemotron stream: compute only the mel frames the encoder can consume
    
    step() recomputed the log-mel over the whole buffer, so a stream cost grew
    with the square of its length. It now builds frames [consumed, limit) from
    the samples they cover, keeps only the samples the next step can need, and
    builds the window and filterbank once per session.
    
    Every frame matches the whole-buffer mel bit for bit:
    - MLX's float RFFT packs rows (2i, 2i+1) into one complex FFT and an odd last
      row with itself, so a slice starts on an even frame and ends on an even
      frame or the last one.
    - MLX runs (rows, 257) @ (257, 128) as split-K up to 257 rows, with a
      partition count that depends on rows, and as the regular GEMM above. Slices
      are zero-padded to 258 rows so they take the regular GEMM, whose K order
      does not depend on the row count. Below 258 mel frames the session computes
      the whole-buffer mel as before and does not trim the sample buffer.
    - Frame counts use stft's formula; with hop > nFft the trim point is clamped
      to the buffer.
    Pre-emphasis, windowing and log are elementwise.
    incrementalMelFramesMatchFullMelBitForBit checks 1 s to 90 s prefixes with
    slices of 2, 30, 114 and 258 rows on Metal.
    
    * Nemotron stream: skip steps that cannot fill an encoder chunk
    
    When fewer than a chunk's mel frames are ready, streamEncodeChunks encodes nothing. The step now returns before computing the mel and rebuilding the transcript text, which gave an empty delta anyway.
    
    ---------
    
    Co-authored-by: Arvind Chembarpu <achembarpu@gmail.com>

commit a6c7289237b149ec51e067220cc8738cc7303997
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 21 08:12:15 2026 -0700

    VoxtralRealtime streaming encoder: write KV rows into preallocated storage (#272)
    
    Each streaming step concatenated the new keys and values onto the cached
    window in every encoder layer, so every step copied up to slidingWindow rows
    per layer. Each layer now has a cache that writes new rows with a slice
    update, and attention reads the filled prefix: the same rows in the same
    order. The storage grows 256 rows at a time up to the window. The session
    resets the caches at each window boundary instead of dropping them, so the
    next window reuses the storage.
    
    The encoder also casts the RoPE tables to the q/k dtype once per pass
    instead of twice per layer.
    
    Voxtral Mini 4B Realtime on an M-series MacBook Pro, 2 minutes of speech in
    100 ms steps: max MLX footprint (active plus cache) 5 191 -> 4 009 MB, mean
    step unchanged at 61.7 ms, transcript byte-identical.

